      "text": "Wamsutter is the largest settlement and the only incorporated town in the Great Divide Basin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wamsutter%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Known as the Gateway to the Red Desert, Wamsutter was founded in 1868 as a vital service stop for the Union Pacific Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Wamsutter,_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Originally named Washakie after a Shoshone chief, the town was renamed in 1884 for Union Pacific engineer James Wamsutter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Wamsutter,_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area around Wamsutter was originally inhabited by the Shoshone and Ute tribes, who hunted bison, deer, and antelope here.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Wamsutter,_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wamsutter provided an essential oasis for steam locomotives that required regular stops for water and coal in the arid Red Desert.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
